#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147288 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7bad79c3-d90e-5752-a3ea-381e9445ab15" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations that Represent Simple Harmonic Motion"}] :question/id #uuid "54c15528-aec0-5554-b3b0-f928f3a6d3d5" :question/math-academy-id "e-6619" :question/problem "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t$. Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing simple harmonic motion?\n\n1. $y'' + 81y = 0$\n2. $y'' + 9y'+81y = 0$\n3. $y'' - 81y = 0$" :question/worked-solution "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = 0\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- Equation I takes the form\n$y'' + \\omega^2 y = 0$\nwith\n$\\omega^2 = 81 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\omega = 9$.\nTherefore, equation I represents SHM.\n- Equation II does not take the form\n$y'' + \\omega^2 y = 0$\nbecause it has a $y'$ term.\n- Equation III does not take the form\n$y'' + \\omega^2 y = 0$.\nIt would require $\\omega^2 = -81$, which is not possible for any real number $\\omega$ since $\\omega^2 \\geq 0$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147289 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef654722-031d-51cc-86fc-a0cbc498b350" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Period of a Simple Harmonic Oscillation"}] :question/id #uuid "4c6cb7ad-3d0d-5d85-bda7-a9c8874ce25b" :question/math-academy-id "e-6620" :question/problem "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$y'' + 100y = 0$,\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the oscillation?" :question/worked-solution "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = 0\n$$\n\nwhere the period $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\omega}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 100\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\omega = 10\n$$\n\nso the period (in seconds) is\n\n$$\nT = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{10} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{5}\\,\\textrm{s}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147290 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e17fe7fc-3b85-5f2c-aa69-2336b05b16b2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Simple Harmonic Motion Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "2ca66468-091b-5f69-99eb-2d347486becd" :question/math-academy-id "e-6621" :question/problem "A particle is moving with simple harmonic motion. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$y'' + 9 y = 0$.\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)$?\n\n1. $y = R\\cos(3t+\\phi_1)$\n2. $y = R\\sin(9t+\\phi_2)$\n3. $y = A\\cos{3t} + B\\sin{3t}$\n\nNote that $R,\\phi_1,\\phi_2, A$ and $B$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "The SHM equation is\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2y = 0\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1. $y = R\\cos{(\\omega t + \\phi_1)}$\n2. $y = R\\sin{(\\omega t + \\phi_2)}$\n3. $y =A\\cos{\\omega t} + B\\sin{\\omega t}$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 9\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\omega = 3\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\omega=3$ into the three different forms of the general solution gives the following:\n\n1. $y = R\\cos{(3t + \\phi_1)}$\n2. $y = R\\sin{(3t + \\phi_2)}$\n3. $y =A\\cos{3t} + B\\sin{3 t}$\n\nTherefore, only functions I and III could be used to represent $y(t)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147291 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2dedcee-452d-50ff-844e-5648be3066b4" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Amplitude of an Simple Harmonic Oscillation"}] :question/id #uuid "2e1c98d9-bce3-5b6a-bbef-ee32fa2a2448" :question/math-academy-id "e-6622" :question/problem "The position $y(t)$, measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion, is governed by the equation\n\n$y'' + 36y = 0$,\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with a velocity of $3\\,\\textrm{m/s}$, find the amplitude of the oscillation." :question/worked-solution "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = 0\n$$\n\nand the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\ny = R\\sin{(\\omega t + \\phi)}\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the amplitude and $\\phi$ is the phase.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 36\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\omega = 6\n$$\n\nso the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\ny = R\\sin{(6t+\\phi)}\n$$\n\nWe're given the initial condition $y(0) = 0$. Substituting this into the equation above gives\n\n$$\nR\\sin\\phi = 0\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\phi = 0\n$$\n\nSince $\\phi=0$, our general solution reduces to\n\n$$\ny = R\\sin{6 t}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the velocity is $3\\,\\textrm{m/s}$ when $t=0$, which means that $y'(0) = 3$. Differentiating the general solution with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\ny' = 6R\\cos{6t}\n$$\n\nand substituting $y'(0) = 3$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n3 = 6R\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad R=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the amplitude is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\\,\\textrm{m}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147292 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a459ec25-988e-5f91-8c25-2b7b81b6b6ee" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of a Mass-Spring System"}] :question/id #uuid "7489abc3-36e8-5c4d-a2cf-a2bcb14b63ed" :question/math-academy-id "e-6623" :question/problem "A particle of mass $36\\,\\textrm{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k = 9\\, \\textrm{kg/s}^2$. The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds.\n\nIf the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, then which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The governing differential equation is $y'' + \\dfrac{1}{2}y = 0$\n2. The frequency $f$ of the oscillation is $f = \\dfrac{1}{4\\pi}\\,\\textrm{s}^{-1}$\n3. The general solution can be expressed as $y = R\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}t+\\phi\\right)}$, where $R$ is the amplitude and $\\phi$ is the phase." :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m=36$ moving with simple harmonic motion (SHM) under the action of a spring with spring constant $k=9$ has\n$\\omega^2 = \\dfrac{k}{m} = \\dfrac{9}{36} = \\dfrac{1}{4}$,\nso its equation of motion is\n$y'' + \\dfrac{1}{4}y = 0$,\nwhich is different from the equation in statement I.\n- Statement II is true. The frequency $f$ (measured in $s^{-1}$) is given by\n$f = \\dfrac{\\omega}{2\\pi} = \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)}{2\\pi} = \\dfrac{1}{4\\pi} \\,\\textrm{s}^{-1}. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n- Statement III is true. The general solution for $y(t)$ can take one of three forms:\n$A\\cos ωt + B\\sin ωt, R\\cos (ωt + ϕ_{1}), R\\sin (ωt + ϕ_{2})$.\nWriting $\\omega = \\dfrac{1}{2}$ and $\\phi_1 = \\phi$ in the form $R\\sin{(\\omega t+\\phi_2)}$ gives\n$y(t) = R\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}t+\\phi\\right)}. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148077 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7bad79c3-d90e-5752-a3ea-381e9445ab15" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations that Represent Simple Harmonic Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8c3b9be-b025-58e7-953a-51cd756d15e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 4y^{′} = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146474} {:answer/id #uuid "abd0ed18-0fa2-55e1-b19a-ae8ceaac686b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 4y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146475} {:answer/id #uuid "cc1a3548-b49e-58b0-a316-007a0d95f542" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 4y^{2} = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146476} {:answer/id #uuid "e7437f43-77d1-51f2-be49-53187f6f5bf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} - 4y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146477} {:answer/id #uuid "ec4d3563-a2a7-5116-ba77-a52069fb7e4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 2y^{′} + 4y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146478}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abd0ed18-0fa2-55e1-b19a-ae8ceaac686b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 4y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146475} :answer-field/id #uuid "3bb5f331-164d-50b0-b86d-c194735fcfdd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146473}] :question/id #uuid "916caca2-b090-554c-9711-817f0993a96f" :question/math-academy-id "q-64217" :question/problem "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t$. Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing simple harmonic motion?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148079 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef654722-031d-51cc-86fc-a0cbc498b350" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Period of a Simple Harmonic Oscillation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97ddf765-6ff6-5313-af16-a5b29b0329d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{4}s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146486} {:answer/id #uuid "97e23f17-e280-5211-bdc5-666bf373939a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2πs" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146487} {:answer/id #uuid "1bdc0645-0107-5fde-9ed0-38b1df5a35a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{2}s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146488} {:answer/id #uuid "33c126a9-d76e-5c49-bde0-d45c20e08ab3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{8}s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146489} {:answer/id #uuid "5d666e96-6aca-5760-a363-950a3b1b2a24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{16}s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146490}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1bdc0645-0107-5fde-9ed0-38b1df5a35a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{2}s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146488} :answer-field/id #uuid "b14b9e37-a626-5676-8ad5-9f2457873f96" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146485}] :question/id #uuid "3709cb88-641b-50e9-b4b6-d0a4c2087fba" :question/math-academy-id "q-64221" :question/problem "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$y^{″} + 16y = 0$,\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the oscillation?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148081 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a459ec25-988e-5f91-8c25-2b7b81b6b6ee" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of a Mass-Spring System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6c6414f-229a-5c87-b7db-2c91aef0b4c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} - 4y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146498} {:answer/id #uuid "e724930a-6e42-58c1-b98c-58d392e1be4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 2y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146499} {:answer/id #uuid "e82548b1-ed84-5294-a253-10d6938fbbaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 4y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146500} {:answer/id #uuid "f3a0b027-083e-54cc-af6a-4dfccb1dcb1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + \\sqrt{2}y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146501} {:answer/id #uuid "16eb5462-cee1-5acd-8093-75a706390312" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} - \\sqrt{2}y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146502}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e724930a-6e42-58c1-b98c-58d392e1be4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 2y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146499} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f0f923c-6682-5c68-9e0d-dd1c6e325664" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146497}] :question/id #uuid "2bb1b50c-fcc3-53d2-91c4-0d8dd7036065" :question/math-academy-id "q-64229" :question/problem "A particle of mass $12kg$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k = 24kg/s^{2}$. The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t > 0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148088 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2dedcee-452d-50ff-844e-5648be3066b4" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Amplitude of an Simple Harmonic Oscillation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d7fe1e7-05fa-5407-906a-6234e021a8d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146540} {:answer/id #uuid "b60c7201-6a6a-53d4-a03f-262c14513316" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146541} {:answer/id #uuid "d49102b9-9608-550d-a7ea-f0656947d2a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146542} {:answer/id #uuid "ea9e440f-ac3a-5d3e-921a-352386e6de10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146543} {:answer/id #uuid "3df3185f-2921-5c98-9fe5-09c7c28e3d8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146544}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b60c7201-6a6a-53d4-a03f-262c14513316" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146541} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c1b78e0-b695-5680-93c7-66bdc6619adb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146539}] :question/id #uuid "012482b3-0bd8-57c6-8936-9b9b3da520e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-64254" :question/problem "The position $y(t)$, measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion, is governed by the equation\n\n$y^{″} + 144y = 0$,\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with a velocity of $8m/s$, find the amplitude of the oscillation."}]}
